Act 1\par
Prologue\par
The fence crackled in excitement as he lit his cigarette, the smoke going through the wire without care. He drove into the clearing with the headlights off as the haze parted before him. The air was wet that night, and Clouds covered the sky sparsely. The night was illuminated by the blur of neon below it. His balaclava hung from his neck, and inhaling the smoke deeply, slowly, and methodically, he ran over his list of assignments for the night. Looking into his green eyes in the rearview mirror, \ldblquote Last time. I swear. Last fucking time\'85\rdblquote  he thought to himself.\par
Wildlife was muffled in the background; he could hear the familiar sounds of sirens in the distance buzzing wildly. He unbuckled his seat belt as the car came to a halt. Turning it off he jumped out of the jeep and headed towards the boot of it. There were 5 rows of explosives jerry-rigged to a generator, and next to it a briefcase glimmering silver in the moonlight. The dimness of the phone was his only light. \ldblquote Impressive,\rdblquote  he thought to himself.\par
He looked at the fence feeling the static emanating off it. Checking into the app provided, he called his dispatcher. The phone rang for 3 beats before an answer; \ldblquote #11 are you ready to go\rdblquote  the voice was direct and heavy with weight. \ldblquote Yes sir, though its different from the pictures you gave me in the dossier.\rdblquote  He said. \ldblquote You have your orders, complete them and we will give you the cure. There\rquote s a car waiting for you in the building to your right across the field.\rdblquote  The voice was Monotone. The line went dead before he could even answer. \ldblquote Fucking bastard,\rdblquote  he muttered to himself.\par
Inspecting the Contents of the briefcase there were 2 Walther p99 handguns that fit snugly in black foam; there were also 2 magazines, one with normal bullets for one gun, and the other had tranquilizers. He put a Silencer on one and holstered them; taking some electrical gloves out from one of his jacket pockets, he lowered himself connecting the charges into one of the transformers. He stepped back while he saw the Coils begin to glow orange. Running his hand over his brown, slicked-back hair, he pulled the balaclava up again. Taking some distance and allowing himself a few more steps back, he looked on from behind a tree a few hundred feet away. Sparks flew as the explosion blew out the windows. He could feel the heat from where he stood, setting the car ablaze. Moving with one of the pistols aimed forward, he stepped through the mess that once used to be an electric fence.\par
 Lights began to illuminate the fields, and the distant hum of security drones flew across them. The fence surrounding the city went into emergency mode as he moved past a few factory spaces and into the backside of some semi-burned-down apartments. A chattering of radios could be heard in the air: "There\rquote s a breach in one of the sectors!" The radio cut in. "Sergeant, there\rquote s a truck on fire and the gate is wide open," he overheard. Three military police patrolled below. He sat in a corner of the burnt apartment, looking around for the car that was supposed to be there. Pulling out the phone, he dialed the only number on it: "Go ahead, 11." The voice was cold. "I'm in the building; where's the car?" he asked quietly as search drones buzzed overhead scanning the fields."What do you mean, we had it delivered right before you got there?" the voice responded. "Give me a moment," he said. "We'll locate it." The voice had some static to it. "It's not here," he said, #11 immediately weighed his chances of taking it on foot. "Bad luck. It was impounded under an hour ago. It's your choice to continue." The phone cut out again. "It's not like I have much of a choice; I'm already here, guy!" he cursed into the phone.\par
There wasn\rquote t much to do about it; he would have to travel into the city and steal something if he wanted to get out. Traveling a few blocks, following the path on his holo-map, he moved in and out of the empty building structures. The explosion served as a very well-placed distraction. The streets were semi-empty. It was around 3 a.m., and night shift workers were unloading pallets into a truck. He moved in and out of the amber light from the street lamps in the direction indicated by the map, noticing a few security guards huddled over their phones. A few lone cargo vans were lined up near the exit. One of them was on the engine purring listlessly. He went around the bend to get a better view. Another security guard was smoking a cigarette and talking on the phone.he gripped the the pistol that held the tranqs, and he stepped up to the man's neck, startling him "Who the fuck!" He froze at the sight of the gleaming pistol and his consciousness faded to black.\par
 #11 moved him out of the way and began to change outfits. He fumbled with the belt. Checking the pockets of the uniform. He found a plastic card in a plastic pouch. "This badge will make it much more convenient," he thought, rubbing the ID card on the concrete to mask the picture. He managed to get into the building. He watched intently as the guards kept to their corners.\par
The building schematics showed him exactly the routes he needed to take. He was to head to radiology. "I\rquote m so confused," he thought to himself. "Why would they send an insert here of all places?".\par
 He studied the map as a doctor and a nurse walked by. "So she\rquote s not dating him anymore; I thought they were partnered?" said the nurse. "She\rquote s single now; you never know," laughed the doctor, looking in #11\rquote s direction. "Slow night, isn\rquote t it?" The doctor waved at him, taking off his glasses and cleaning them with his lab coat. "The way I like it; too bad the cafeteria is closed now," #11 remarked he calmly walked off following the map's direction and entered a lab space with the stolen ID card. The floors were bright white, and the halls were empty. He moved quickly and efficiently.\par
The deeper he went into the building, the colder it was. He figured it would be best to stay out of people's way and away from open spaces with personnel; the research facility shown brightly on his holo map. Using the stairwell, he was directed toward the lower levels. "What could be there\f1\emdash chemicals, research data?" he thought. He lingered a bit too long, and one of the doctors broke off and walked over to him from the front desk. "This area is a quarantine zone for the R & D department, it's after hours; is there something going on?" "Yes, we had a silent alarm go off; is everything okay?" he said. He was quick on his feet, cursing himself for missing a beat. She gave him a look, and he couldn't help but smile coyly.\rdblquote its probably just a false alarm, mind if I take a look around\rdblquote  he said \ldblquote yea sure you Military Police are always walking around\rdblquote  she smiled coyly back at him. His phone rang, startling him \ldblquote mind if I take this\rdblquote  he said.\rdblquote yea see ya around\rdblquote  she said. He walked off, turning into another Lab Space. "You are to bring her to the designated location; I will personally meet you," said the deep voice. His mind raced\emdash could it be a doctor or a patient?. "You need to give me more information now," he said. "What the fuck is going on?" He got louder than he should have. \ldblquote We will create a diversion, we will create a space of time for you to get out\rdblquote  The line went dead. As soon as he finished, he noticed personnel running back and forth behind him and over the PA systems\emdash all sorts of emergency codes.\par
His heart pounded as he ran down the narrow corridor, following the directions on the holographic map. \ldblquote Room 10,\rdblquote  it displayed, pointing ahead. As he got closer, he noticed the nametags on the doors\emdash Experiment 16, Experiment 15, and Experiment 11 stopped him cold: \ldblquote Experiment 11: Matter.\rdblquote  His last name, his designation? A creeping sense of dread rose in his chest.\par
Shaking off the unease, he moved on and reached Room 10. The nametag read \ldblquote Experiment 10: Cosma.\rdblquote  He peered through the small window, his breath fogging the glass. Inside, the room was bathed in a dim, ambient red light, casting unsettling shadows that seemed to shift on their own.\par
The lights in the corridors flickered off and the alarm sounds were deafening. He heard rustling and looked behind him; a sudden impact knocked him to the ground. There was a man in military uniform holding a rifle. "Who the fuck are you?" the man roared. "You're not supposed to be here!" he said. He jumped from the floor and pushed the man as he fired close to his ear, the muzzle flash blinding the darkness and sending his train of thought into a belligerent haze.He assessed the situation, his ears ringing; it was dark; and the man punched him so hard that he lost his breath. He managed to force the gun from his grip. The man yelled into his radio "I need help; send someone to R&D!". #11 noticed the glimmer of the rifle, and muscle memory acted before he even thought of anything. Sliding his hand into the handle of his silenced pistol, he heard two muffled shots and one loud one. All he could hear were wet sounds.\par
The man was dead before he even hit the floor. He stumbled backward, white-hot pain shooting through his hand as he tried to push himself up, clutching his limp arm. Desperately, he tore off his uniform, revealing the wound. His forearm\rquote s muscle was stained red, but thankfully, it had only grazed him. The sharp pain hit once the numbness from the endorphins and adrenaline faded. Blood oozed from the wound, the chaos of blaring sounds still echoing around him. Lightheaded and nauseous, he vomited whatever little was left in his stomach. Struggling to steady himself, he fashioned a tourniquet from a piece of his torn garment and, gritting his teeth, stumbled back to the room.\par
He stood in front of the room the holo-map had directed him to, trying to use his stolen ID, but it was useless. Frustrated, he resorted to kicking the door and firing three shots into the passkey lock\emdash he had no idea what the password was. His holo-map shut off, and in its place, a holographic silhouette made of code appeared in his hand. He dropped the phone, confused by what he saw.\par
\ldblquote Pick me up; I work with the boss,\rdblquote  the figure said.\par
He hesitated, but then held the phone in his palm.\par
\ldblquote Point me toward the door,\rdblquote  it instructed.\par
\ldblquote Why did you shoot it? You missed it anyway.\rdblquote  it mocked.  \ldblquote What are you?\rdblquote  #11 poked the phone with his thumb.\par
The latch turned, the lock clicked, and the door opened, releasing a blast of freezing air. He had no idea what was going on.\par
He\rquote d faced danger countless times as a mercenary, but this felt different\emdash this wasn\rquote t like any operation he had been on before.\par
The room was small, barely a few feet across, dark and unsettling. But he stepped inside, his pistol raised, scanning the room for any sign of danger.\par
 He put it back in the holster and looked at a file hanging from the door. It was filled with notes and codes. He didn\rquote t understand  "Experiment #10: Mary Cosma," It read in Bold Letters. He Flipped through the pages "no signs of progress or antibody production from the last failed experiment. If no improvements are shown, scrap the experiment and start over with experiment 11." He looked over, surprised by the light. A machine next to him turned on, and he heard the loud mechanical sounds of motors running. He walked over to it. It overtook him, losing all sense of time, space, and reason\emdash anger, confusion, sadness, whatever it was he was baffled. "Specimen code: Mary Cosma -10 years old- Vitals stable; heart rate with slight palpitations; due to new vaccine and virus interactions," the silhouette from his phone said. He felt disgusted looking at the papers; they were experimenting on people. He looked behind him across the hall and saw "Experiment 20." They must have had adults in there too. He returned her stare. She was terrified, and her teeth chattered. He took a blanket from one of the tables next to him and draped it over her. "I'm here to help," he said, his breath freezing the air, and he could tell they'd been sedated for a long period of time. He wanted to help the others; he needed to, but 5 minutes wasn't enough time, and they may have killed them due to the infiltration. He tried to pull her, but she held her ground, shaking her head. He could see fear in her eyes. Sadness rolled over. "Please let me help you," he said, holding out his only good hand as she sat upright. She shook her head again and pointed to her leg; it was shackled. He touched the cold wire leading to the ground. "Will you really?" she said with a weak voice. "I promise I'll get you out of here." "You have my word," said #11, hugging her. "Wait! My friend next door\emdash we have to help him too. "Please, we'll talk through that crack in the wall," she sobbed, pointing to it, and he froze. "Dave Matter" was his son.\par
